      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e It looks to me like a function that causes the system to panic based on a reason that you give it. Is my understanding correct?\n\nNo. From the link you provided:\n```\n/**\n * @brief Fatal error policy handler\n *\n * This function is not invoked by application code, but is declared as a\n * weak symbol so that applications may introduce their own policy.\n *\n * The default implementation of this function halts the system\n * unconditionally. Depending on architecture support, this may be\n * a simple infinite loop, power off the hardware, or exit an emulator.\n *\n * If this function returns, then the currently executing thread will be\n * aborted.\n```\n\nThis function implements fatal error policy. By default it reboots the system, but it can be changed. In test we can use it to detect crashes.\n\nYou can use `z_except_reason()` to panic with some specific error code.\n\n\u003e Can you elaborate on how this function is being called?\n\n\u003e is it because k_sys_fatal_error_handler() is designed to be invoked by the system when a fatal error occurs, rather than being called directly?\n\nYes. Finding path from `k_thread_abort()` to `k_sys_fatal_error_handler()` was left as an exercise for interested reader 😊",
      "parentUuid": "4b0f9e40_1954067d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 25,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 25,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "ee643ec59021565636d3df28e08079b3504fc333",
